+\paragraph{\textcolor{blue}{Sensitivity to recycle delay.}}\label{par:cstr-delay-sensitivity}
+\rev{The recycle delay is varied over $\tau\in\{0.010,0.025,0.050\}\,\mathrm{h}$, corresponding to 2, 5, and 10 sampling intervals. At each delay, all six predictors are fitted separately using 50 training and 12 validation trajectories. Initial states, heat-input sequences, and process-noise realizations are paired across delays by using the same generation seeds. The integration and sampling intervals, physical parameters, network architectures, history and memory lengths, optimization settings, and neural training seed remain unchanged. The training-state and input normalization scales from the original $0.025\,\mathrm{h}$ dataset are retained throughout, so errors at different delays use the same coordinate scales.}
+
+\rev{For DLM--K, the physical delay is not an explicit model input or a separately optimized scalar: the learned weights, including the lifting, linear-core, and memory parameters, are re-estimated from data at each delay. Its architecture retains 162,747 trainable parameters in all three cases. Delay--RBF receives the corresponding known lag of 2, 5, or 10 samples; its dictionary size, kernel width, ridge coefficient, and construction seed are fixed. After all fitted checkpoints are frozen, 12 new paired test trajectories per delay are generated. Evaluation uses the same 882 prediction origins per trajectory and the error definition in Equation~\eqref{eq:error}, with all trajectories retained. Thus, this experiment assesses performance after identification at each fixed delay.}
+
+\rev{DLM--K has the lowest mean error among the six predictors at all six pre-specified reporting steps for each delay. Its step-60 error increases from 0.004849 to 0.006862 and 0.01066 as the delay increases (Table~\ref{tab:cstr-delay-sensitivity}). These errors are 45.0\%, 64.9\%, and 55.7\% below the corresponding Delay--RBF errors, the lowest among the baselines at this step. The error curves in Figure~\ref{fig:cstr-delay-sensitivity} show that the advantage persists beyond the 30-step training horizon. Delay--RBF has lower errors at steps 2--4 for $0.010\,\mathrm{h}$ and steps 2--3 for $0.050\,\mathrm{h}$; DLM--K has the lowest error from step 5 through step 60 at all three delays. The direction of the delay dependence is model-specific: the three neural baselines have lower step-60 errors at longer delays, whereas DLM--K and the two RBF predictors have higher errors. Consequently, increasing the delay does not impose a common monotonic accuracy trend across predictor families, even under paired forcing and fixed model capacities.}
