 \subsection{Discussion and limitations}\label{sec:discussion}
 
-The independent simulated tests evaluate the frozen predictors on previously unused trajectories generated under the specified delays and operating rules. They provide evidence of generalization to new draws from these simulation distributions, rather than to different delays, broader operating regimes, or physical plant measurements. Variability across independently trained realizations was not assessed. The separate post-hoc CSTR diagnostics describe condition-dependent behavior and are not additional independent tests.
+\rev{The independent simulated tests evaluate new trajectories at specified fixed delays. The additional CSTR experiment extends this evaluation to three separately fitted delay conditions. All three delays lie within the $0.095\,\mathrm{h}$ span of the 20 observed states. This makes the delayed separator state available in the recorded history, but covering the delay alone establishes neither an approximation-error bound for the learned lift nor stability of the nonlinear memory-dependent recurrence. Prediction accuracy is therefore supported empirically for the tested conditions and horizons; a quantitative guarantee or transfer of a fixed predictor to an unseen delay is not established. Variability across independently trained realizations was not assessed. The separate post-hoc CSTR diagnostics describe condition-dependent behavior and are not additional independent tests.}
 
 The comparisons concern complete predictors with different architectures, parameter counts, and optimization budgets. They do not isolate the marginal effect of current-state conditioning, LSTM encoding, HaKAN lifting, or AFT correction. The RBF references are frozen closed-form fits and do not receive the neural models' current-state projection or staged optimization. No direct comparison with the Koopman+AFT autoencoder of \citet{Nagdi2026AFTKoopman} is reported. Regularization of the linear core does not establish stability of the nonlinear memory-dependent predictor.
 
